Let play start at a speed, exactly as the speed command sets it

A customer starting a sound and setting its speed had to send a play and
then a speed command, and the speed command raced the play's background
load and was silently dropped. Starting the sound at its speed removes
the need for the second command in that case. (The ordering race itself
is fixed separately.)

play now takes speed and pitch_correction with the speed command's
meaning, ranges and defaults (1.0, no correction), in the flattened and
nested message forms and on the typed POST /play endpoint:

- A full-load play sets the speed on the sample before it is handed to
  the audio thread, through the same set_speed_with_mode a speed command
  ends up in, so the corrector is built off the audio thread and the
  status snapshot reports the starting speed.
- Pitch correction on a cold play whose progressive load is still
  filling stays dormant until the D51 upgrade, as it does for a speed
  command, and logs the same "deferred" warning; the warning now lives
  in one helper both paths call.
- A windowed play plays forward only, so it starts at normal speed and
  logs a warning, matching what a speed command on a streamed voice
  does.
- speed 0, and a negative speed with pitch_correction, reject the play
  at parse time (HTTP 400). The speed-0 rejection is shared with the
  speed command instead of repeating its message.

Audited the other per-sample controls the audio engine accepts for any
that play could not already set: seek has start_position_ms, volume has
volume, fades have fade_in and routing gains have channel_map;
voice_volume changes state shared by every sound in the voice rather
than the new sound, so it stays a command. Speed and pitch were the only
gap.

Docs: the play table in commands.md, a "Start Speed" section in the
playback-control guide, the typed-endpoint notes in http-api.md and the
web UI API contract (both also claimed channel_map was not accepted by
typed /play, which stopped being true when main's channel_map-over-HTTP
change was merged), and the changelog.

Tests (written first, each failing before the change): parse of speed
and pitch_correction in flattened and legacy nested soundPlay forms, the
1.0/false defaults, reverse speed accepted without correction, speed 0
and reverse-with-correction rejected; dispatch starts the sample at the
speed and reports it in status; a warm play with pitch correction starts
with a corrector; a still-loading play with pitch correction warns that
it is deferred; a windowed play with a speed warns and plays at normal
speed while an explicit 1.0 does not warn; a reverse play starts at its
start position running backwards; typed POST /play forwards both fields
in a form parse_command accepts.

Validation: cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings,
and a warning-free release build (RUSTFLAGS=-D warnings); fuzz suite 7
passed; broker-backed native suite (MQTTAUDIO_BROKER_TESTS=1, TLS CA
set) 958 passed, 0 failed, 7 device-gated ignored.

